WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
SQL injection remains a common example because the attack changes the meaning of a

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.340
database query.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.600
The same pattern can appear anywhere user input is mixed into an interpreter without

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.260
strong boundaries.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.360
Command injection is especially dangerous because it can let an attacker execute operating

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.260
system commands through application features such as file conversion,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.640
diagnostics, import jobs, or administrative utilities.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.180
LDAP, NoSQL, template, expression language,

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.360
and other injection types follow the same core failure:

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:39.220
data crosses a trust boundary and becomes executable or structural input.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.640
Output encoding and input validation are complementary controls.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.980
Validation rejects input that does not belong,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.000
while encoding and parameterization preserve the boundary between data and instructions.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.380
Use parameterized queries and safe APIs instead of string-built commands.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.400
Validate input against expected formats and ranges.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.860
Encode output for the correct context and handle errors without leaking internals.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:08.520
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SI-10 - Information Input Validation;

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.580
SI-11 - Error Handling; SI-7 - Software, Firmware, and Information Integrity.
